WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.939
Webhook receivers often need to acknowledge quickly.

2
00:00:02.939 --> 00:00:06.964
Providers may expect a timely response and retry when the consumer is
slow.

3
00:00:06.964 --> 00:00:13.098
A common pattern is to validate the request, record the event, and place
work on a queue for background processing.

4
00:00:13.098 --> 00:00:17.443
This separates the public HTTP acknowledgement path from slower business
work.

5
00:00:17.443 --> 00:00:26.707
The handler can remain small and predictable while workers perform
retries, database updates, notifications, or downstream API calls with
better operational controls.

6
00:00:26.707 --> 00:00:29.327
Queueing does not remove security obligations.

7
00:00:29.327 --> 00:00:38.911
Validation should happen before enqueueing enough information to trust the
event, or the queued record should clearly preserve an untrusted state
until validation completes.

8
00:00:38.911 --> 00:00:46.641
Workers need the same tenant mapping, authorization checks, idempotency
records, and payload validation expectations as synchronous code.

9
00:00:46.641 --> 00:00:55.331
A queue can improve reliability, but it can also spread unsafe assumptions
if the event is treated as trusted merely because it reached internal
infrastructure.

10
00:00:55.331 --> 00:00:57.120
Transaction boundaries matter.

11
00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:05.745
If the handler writes a deduplication record, updates a database, and
triggers a downstream action, the order and atomicity of those steps
affect correctness.

12
00:01:05.745 --> 00:01:11.751
Inbox and outbox pattern concepts can help preserve durable state about
received events and outgoing actions.

13
00:01:11.751 --> 00:01:17.438
Dead-letter queues can help operators inspect events that cannot be
processed after repeated attempts.

14
00:01:17.438 --> 00:01:23.635
These patterns are not just reliability tools; they reduce the chance of
hidden data loss or repeated side effects.

15
00:01:23.635 --> 00:01:27.213
Ordering, partial failure, and backpressure should be explicit.

16
00:01:27.213 --> 00:01:34.433
Some providers do not guarantee event order, and even when order is
intended, retries and parallel workers can rearrange processing.

17
00:01:34.433 --> 00:01:40.695
A secure system should avoid assuming that every event arrives once, in
order, and with all dependencies available.

18
00:01:40.695 --> 00:01:49.320
Backpressure controls, worker limits, retry bounds, and monitoring help
the application remain stable when event volume spikes or downstream
services fail.
